**The 1-D histogram did not fail because of binning.** Adversarial AUC = 1.00 means val and test are separable, so \(p_\text{te}/p_\text{val}\) does not exist. Importance-weighted CV is then undefined, not mis-tuned ([Cortes et al. 2010](https://papers.nips.cc/paper/4156-learning-bounds-for-importance-weighting); [Sugiyama et al. 2007](https://jmlr.org/papers/v8/sugiyama07a.html)). Clipping to \([0.2,5]\) does not create the ratio.

**Established substitute:** select on CVaR / worst-group of squared error (mean SE on the worst 5%, which already holds 45% of SSE), or rebuild val from the most test-like labeled rows ([FastML Numerai](https://fastml.com/adversarial-validation-part-two): that reversal matched private LB). If no val row looks like test, public LB is the only unbiased probe of \(P_\text{te}\).

**This contest is AmbrosM, not Porto:** public and private are a split of the same 3868 rows, so public ranks \(P_\text{te}\) and val does not. When overall-val \(\Delta\) and public \(\Delta\) disagree, pick the card that wins the tail group, not the lower overall val RMSE.

**Do not fit a new CV→LB slope.** A 2-parameter OLS needs \(\gtrsim\) 20–30 same-axis pairs ([Harrell](https://www.revespcardiol.org/en-regression-modeling-strategies-articulo-S1885585711002726)); 6 already flipped \(a\) from 2.08 to 0.768. With 8 slots left, pre-register one tail/rebuilt-val selector and burn 1–2 slots as confirmatory public tests, not as more regression points.
